augustine-3.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: augustine-3.logic.r2 and augustine-3.logic.r2.2 have "unstable"; augustine-3.logic.r2.3 has "unstable" and "unstable → ¬understand"; they differ when only "they understand how the things be made which by and in the Wisdom of God are made" and "their heart fluttereth between the motions of things past and to come and is still unstable" are true, which the first allows
augustine-3.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps augustine-3.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "they understand how the things be made which by and in the Wisdom of God are made" and "their heart fluttereth between the motions of things past and to come and is still unstable" are true. Is S1, "Who speak thus, do not yet understand Thee, O Wisdom of God, Light of souls, understand not yet how the things be made, which by Thee, and in Thee are made: yet they strive to comprehend things eternal, whilst their heart fluttereth between the motions of things past and to come, and is still unstable.", true then?
augustine-3.md:1: argue/unfaithful: augustine-3.logic.r2 P5 (S3): the sentence has no word of "they understand how the things be made which by and in the Wisdom of God are made": is it the sentence P5 came from?
augustine-3.md:1: argue/negation: augustine-3.logic.r2 P5 (S3): the sentence says "neither", but P5 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if they understand how the things be made which by and in the Wisdom of God are made, then the heart of man is held so that it stands still"
